Subject: sysupgrade: no provisioning volume on the BE7000

Since OpenWrt 725a1dc534 sysupgrade creates a provisioning UBI volume by
default. On the BE7000 it is created before the kernel volume and takes
volume 0, where the stock bootloader looks for the kernel, so an upgraded
slot does not boot any more: amber LED, and only after seven power
cycles the bootloader falls back to the other slot. Seen upgrading a
local build from a 1.4.0 candidate; images written from a factory.ubi or
upgraded from 1.3.x (old sysupgrade) were fine.

---
diff --git a/target/linux/qualcommbe/ipq95xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh b/target/linux/qualcommbe/ipq95xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh
index df15bd4914..647f52d1de 100644
--- a/target/linux/qualcommbe/ipq95xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh
+++ b/target/linux/qualcommbe/ipq95xx/base-files/lib/upgrade/platform.sh
@@ -55,6 +55,11 @@ be7000_do_upgrade() {
 	echo "Writing OpenWrt image to Xiaomi OpenWrt UBI slot $target_part (slot $target_slot)"
 	CI_UBIPART="$target_part"
 	CI_ROOTPART="ubi_rootfs"
+	# sysupgrade creates a "provisioning" volume by default since OpenWrt
+	# 725a1dc534. It is made before the kernel volume and takes volume 0,
+	# and the stock bootloader loads the kernel from volume 0: the slot
+	# never boots again (amber LED until the bootloader gives up on it).
+	UPGRADE_OPT_ADD_PROVISIONING=0
 	nand_detach_ubi "$target_part" || return 1
 	ubiformat "/dev/mtd$mtdnum" -y || return 1
 	nand_do_flash_file "$image" "fwtool -q -i /tmp/sysupgrade.meta -T $image" || return 1
